{
  "id": "09-forgive-the-neighbor",
  "title": "Forgive Without Denying the Wound",
  "tenet": "Leave justice with God; practice mercy",
  "durationMs": 77688,
  "audioAsset": "kingdom/09-forgive-the-neighbor.mp3",
  "transcriptAsset": "assets/kingdom/09-forgive-the-neighbor.json",
  "text": "What if forgiving a wrong did not require you to call it right?\n\nA girl lent her neighbor a lamp for an evening gathering. He broke it, hid the pieces, and told her it had already been cracked. She was hurt more by the lie than by the glass. For days she imagined a way to embarrass him. Then she told him plainly what she knew and asked him to make it right. He offered to repair the lamp. She accepted the repair, but did not lend him another precious thing while trust was being rebuilt. She stopped rehearsing revenge. Her boundaries stayed.\n\nGod called creation very good, yet the world we inhabit bears real wounds. No wrong is beneath God's notice, but we cannot promise when or how another person's choices will meet their consequences. Justice belongs to God; safety and truthful repair still matter to us. When a neighbor sins against you, forgiveness is an opportunity to receive and extend mercy, not a bargain for a reward. It does not excuse abuse or demand unsafe closeness. What hurt have you been carrying into every new day? Name the wrong without minimizing it. Ask God for freedom from revenge. Seek protection, accountability, and repair where needed. Then take one step toward a heart no longer ruled by the injury.",
